🫲
"Conductor"/en
=== start/en
¶Conduct an imperial fleet with one hand. No keyboard, no buttons — the camera
watches your hand and an eight piece orchestra answers.

Raise your hand and the orchestra fills in, one part at a time, until all eight
are playing. Lower it and they fall away until the melody is alone. Slide left
to slow the march down, right to drive it. However many fingers you hold up
chooses who plays the melody.

Every instruction is printed on the stage, so you can learn it without reading
this.¶

hand: Hand()

¶Height is how much orchestra and sideways is how fast, both read straight off
the hand rather than remembered, so they follow it continuously. Each one is
rounded to a step, so the music only changes when the change is big enough to
hear, and a hand that trembles doesn't rewrite the score twenty times a second.¶
lift: ((hand.place.y + 3m) ÷ 6m).min(1).max(0)
layers: 1 + ((lift · 7).round())

reach: ((hand.place.x + 5m) ÷ 10m).min(1).max(0)
pace: 72 + ((reach · 8).round() · 12)

¶Who plays the melody. A fist and a hand out of frame both count no fingers, so
no fingers keeps whoever was playing — otherwise looking away would change the
tune.¶
featured: 5 … ∆ hand …
	(hand.fingers = 0) ? featured hand.fingers

ƒ voice(count•#)•🔈
	(count = 1) ? 🔈.🎹
	(count = 2) ? 🔈.🎻
	(count = 3) ? 🔈.🪈
	(count = 4) ? 🔈.⚡
	🔈.🎺

ƒ icon(count•#)•''
	(count = 1) ? '🎹'
	(count = 2) ? '🎻'
	(count = 3) ? '🪈'
	(count = 4) ? '⚡'
	'🎺'

¶How loud a part is. Every part stays in the music at all times, so none of
them ever loses its place — the ones above the hand are just turned all the way
down.

Each one is set well below the melody, and all eight together come to less than
twice it. Eight parts at full volume don't make a loud orchestra, they make a
muddy one, because everything sounding at once has to fit through the same
speaker.¶
ƒ heard(rank•# loud•%)•% (rank ≤ layers) ? loud 0%

¶The Imperial March, eight bars, one bar to a line. It's written on a chromatic
scale so its out of key notes — the flat second, and the chromatic slide in the
second half — can be written at all: degree 1 is the tonic and every step up is
one semitone, so these numbers are intervals rather than scale steps.

The note values carry the rhythm. A bare number lasts the track's beat, a
quarter note here, and any other length says so on the number itself: 𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 for the
dotted eighth and 𝅘𝅥𝅯 for the sixteenth that snap the march forward, 𝅗𝅥 for the
half note it settles on. Every line adds up to four beats.¶
march: [
	5 5 5 1𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 8𝅘𝅥𝅯
	5 1𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 8𝅘𝅥𝅯 5𝅗𝅥
	12 12 12 13𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 8𝅘𝅥𝅯
	4 1𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 8𝅘𝅥𝅯 5𝅗𝅥
	17 5𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 5𝅘𝅥𝅯 17 16𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 15𝅘𝅥𝅯
	14𝅘𝅥𝅯 13𝅘𝅥𝅯 14 6𝅘𝅥𝅮 11 9𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 8𝅘𝅥𝅯
	7𝅘𝅥𝅯 6𝅘𝅥𝅯 7 1𝅘𝅥𝅮 4 1𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 4𝅘𝅥𝅯
	8 5𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 8𝅘𝅥𝅯 12𝅗𝅥
]

¶Four bars of root notes under it, marching in quarters. It's shorter than the
melody, and every track loops on its own length, so these four bars come around
twice for the melody's eight.¶
bass: [
	5 5 5 5
	5 5 5 5
	1 1 1 1
	5 5 12 8
]

¶A single bar of drums, in the melody's own rhythm. Degrees pick out of a kit
rather than a scale on an instrument with no pitch, so 1 is the bass drum, 2 the
snare, 3 the hi-hat and 5 the tom.¶
drums: [{1 5} {1 5} {1 5} {2𝅘𝅥𝅮𝅭 3} 2𝅘𝅥𝅯]

¶Chords on the afterbeats, the shove that keeps a march from sitting still. The
third bar leans to the minor chord a step and a half below, which is where the
melody's flat notes come from.¶
strings: [
	ø {5 8 12} ø {5 8 12}
	ø {5 8 12} ø {5 8 12}
	ø {1 4 8} ø {1 4 8}
	ø {5 8 12} ø {5 8 12}
]

¶The same four bars held down as bare fifths, one to a bar. A fifth has no
third in it, so it stays out of the way of the chromatic notes overhead.¶
pad: [{5 12} {5 12} {1 8} {5 12}]

¶A bell on the first bar and the third. Its beat is a whole note, so a rest
here is a whole bar of silence.¶
bells: [5 ø 1 ø]

¶A high answer in the gaps, where the melody is holding still.¶
counter: [
	ø ø ø ø
	ø ø 20𝅗𝅥
	ø ø ø ø
	ø ø 17𝅗𝅥
]

song: 🎼(
	[
		🎶(march instrument: voice(featured) volume: 55% beat: 1𝅘𝅥)
		🎶(
			bass
			instrument: 🔈.synthBass
			beat: 1𝅘𝅥
			key: -24semitones
			volume: heard(2 30%)
		)
		🎶(drums instrument: 🔈.🥁 beat: 1𝅘𝅥 volume: heard(3 20%))
		🎶(
			march
			instrument: 🔈.🎷
			beat: 1𝅘𝅥
			key: -12semitones
			volume: heard(4 22%)
		)
		🎶(
			pad
			instrument: 🔈.synthPad
			beat: 1𝅝
			key: -12semitones
			volume: heard(5 16%)
		)
		🎶(strings instrument: 🔈.🎻 beat: 1𝅘𝅥 volume: heard(6 18%))
		🎶(
			bells
			instrument: 🔈.🔔
			beat: 1𝅝
			key: 12semitones
			volume: heard(7 12%)
		)
		🎶(counter instrument: 🔈.🪈 beat: 1𝅘𝅥 volume: heard(8 14%))
	]
	tempo: pace · 1beats/min
	scale: 🎼.chromatic
	name: 'imperial march'
)

¶How far the fleet has travelled. It counts its own ticks rather than reading
the clock, and counts them faster when the march is faster, so the ships fly
with the tempo.¶
travel: 0 … ∆ Time(40ms) … travel + (pace ÷ 72)

ƒ status()•'' '\layers\ of 8 playing · \pace\ beats a minute'

¶One line of the instructions, dim so the fleet and the featured player read
first.¶
ƒ line(text•'')•Phrase Phrase(text 0.3m color: Color(64% 4 265°))

¶The instructions, stacked to the left edge rather than centred, so the eye can
run straight down them.¶
display: 🔳(
	Stack('<' 0.16m)
	[
		💬(icon(featured) 0.9m color: Color(56% 65 25°))
		💬(status() 0.38m color: Color(70% 25 30°))
		line('raise your hand — more of the orchestra')
		line('lower it — the melody alone')
		line('hand left — slower · hand right — faster')
		line('fingers up — 1 🎹 · 2 🎻 · 3 🪈 · 4 ⚡ · 5 🎺')
	]
)

¶A ship crossing the stage, flying in a band well above the instructions so it
never crosses the text. Its lane sets how high it flies and how far along it
starts, so they never fly in formation.¶
ƒ ship(glyph•'' lane•# speed•#)•Phrase
	Phrase(
		glyph
		0.7m
		place: Place(
			(((((travel · speed) + (lane · 47)) % 200) - 100) · 0.045) · 1m
			((lane · 0.65) + 2.2) · 1m
		)
	)

fleet: [
	ship('🛸' 0 3)
	ship('🚀' 1 2)
	ship('🛰️' 2 4)
	ship('🛸' 3 5)
]

ƒ star(x•# y•# size•#)•Phrase
	Phrase('✦' size · 1m place: Place(x · 1m y · 1m) color: Color(46% 8 275°))

¶The stage zooms to fit whatever is on it, so anything that moves would rescale
the whole picture on every tick and leave the words swimming. The four corner
stars are further out than the ships ever fly and further out than the longest
line of text, so the fit has a frame that never changes and the picture holds
still. The rest are just sky.¶
stars: [
	star(-5.6 -2.3 0.18)
	star(5.6 -2.3 0.18)
	star(-5.6 5 0.18)
	star(5.6 5 0.18)
	star(-4.9 2.1 0.22)
	star(-3.9 3.9 0.14)
	star(-2.2 2.5 0.18)
	star(1.1 3.4 0.14)
	star(2.7 4.4 0.22)
	star(4.6 2.7 0.14)
]

🎭(
	[:stars :fleet display song]
	background: Color(7% 6 275°)
	color: Color(88% 3 265°)
)
